  "chapter_id": "neuroscience--memory-and-forgetting--ch02-how-memories-form",
  "title": "Reconsolidation: Editing Memories on Recall",
  "emoji": "🗃️",
  "color": "#6D28D9",
  "description": "A quick win: understand Reconsolidation: Editing Memories on Recall.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Every time you remember something, the memory becomes temporarily fragile, as if freshly written in wet ink.",
      "visual": "🖋️"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Recalling a stored memory pulls it out of its stable state and reopens it for modification in the brain.",
      "visual": "📂"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "This process is called reconsolidation. The memory must be restabilized, and during that window it can be changed.",
      "visual": "🔓"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Researchers gave rats a drug that blocked reconsolidation after recall, and the original fear memory vanished entirely.",
      "visual": "💊"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Reconsolidation matters because understanding the mechanism changes treatment, learning, and behavior.",
      "visual": "🩺"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Remembering is not read-only. Every recall is a chance to edit the file.",
      "visual": "✏️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What happens to a memory during reconsolidation?",
    "options": [
      "It becomes temporarily unstable and can be modified",
      "It is permanently erased from the brain",
      "It moves to a completely new brain region"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
